op and validate  technologies for studying therapeutic  proteins like monoclonal antibodies and  we're going to use this reference  antibody called the NIST lab for several  examples in the following slides now in  the case of a small molecule drug like  aspirin it's an organic molecule and we  can specify pretty much everything about  it according to its network of chemical  bonds its covalent structure but the  situation for protein therapeutics is  different than that the covalent  structure what we call the primary  sequence of the protein the amino acid  sequence can be determined very  accurately by techniques like mass  spectroscopy however in order for the  protein drug to function properly it has  to be folded into a very specific  structure and it has to stay in that  structure and not aggregate so in the  case of protein structure or what we may  also call high order structure there's a  hierarchy of organization starting with  the primary sequence the secondary  structure small structural elements like  HeLa C's and sheets the overall fold of  the protein and then finally any  complexes that the protein might form in  order for the protein to be safe and  efficacious it has to be folded properly  and it can't be aggregated because if  it's aggregated even in such a way that  preserves its activity it can present a  different shape to the immune system an  unfamiliar shape that will cause an  immune response the immune response  might make that dosage of the medicine  ineffective or it might threaten the  life of the patient  so important challenge and we have to  address it  the difficulty is as we get higher up  this chain this hierarchy of structure  it becomes more difficult to measure the  details of structure and atom level  resolution so we're gonna try to resort  to the analytical methods of NMR to help  us discover the details about high order  structure now in order to best conserve  the structure and stabilize it from  unfolding and aggregation