ese four polysorbate signals so this  is what they look like before the Sierra  pulse sequence is applied this is what a  spectrum looks like when the Sierra  pulse sequence is applied so we've  reduced these four signals by between 80  and 90 percent of their original  intensity and so the tails of these  signals no longer interfere with the  protein parts of the spectrum which is  what we want but when we look at the  spectrum we wondered if now that we've  experimental e reduced the size of these  signals if computationally we can do  more to suppress them and so we applied  a method called smile for spectral  modeling that's used elsewhere to  reconstruct an Tamar spectral data and  used it as a way to build a model of the  excipient signals they're leftover  excipient signals and subtract them from  the spectrum so we can generate a  spectrum clean spectrum that goes from  this where we have the small residual  excipient signals to a spectrum like  this with all the exhibit signals have  been subtracted how does it work and  here's here's the model that we've  subtracted to do the suppression the  smile technique takes a spectrum and  decomposes it as a series of individual  peaks and keeps adding Peaks to the  model until the spectrum has been  completely described  down to whatever desired residual so on  the left-hand side what you see at the  very top is what a particular region of  the spectrum in this case the histidine  excipient signal looks like and then in  the little postage stamp lots on the  right hand side you can see the  collective succession of  terms that are added to try to model  that measured histidine signal and in  the middle on the left you can see the  model as it gets larger and more  complicated to better approximate the  measured signal and then at the bottom  on the left you can see the residuals so  far so we've decomposed our signals of  interest into collections of smaller  signals and we can take them all and  subtract them from